; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SecondRevision - the Direct3D 12 renderer's settings
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; This file was written by the renderer on its first start.
; Setting changes are also dynamically saved here every time.
;
; Introduction
; This is an attempt to create a DirectX 12 renderer for the game.
; There are visual improvements in this renderer, but you
; are not obligated to use them. The Vanilla preset exists
; exactly for this reason.
; Ideally, the ONLY thing you want to edit here is the Preset.
; Recommended is awesome, but set it to INeverAskedForThis
; for that extra oomph... (strong GPU required)
;
; Even though the renderer works with the original game too,
; I highly recommend installing the Revision mod for the game.
; It is an awesome improvement for the game, including stability
; and compatibility.
; It does include modified artwork, but HDTP (new models), New Vision
; and the modified Revision levels can all be turned off in the game,
; if you choose so. So please install the Revision mod!
; It can be found on GOG and Steam too!
;
; Requirements:
; - Windows 11 (it may work on older ones too, but I will never test it,
;   and I don't care... don't use an OS without proper support!)
; - A DirectX 12 capable graphics card (this is a DX12 renderer, after all).
; - An HDR display for the HDR mode.
; - A ray tracing capable GPU for the RT options.
; - The 64-bit companion app for the RT options, FSR4, DLSS, XeSS and
;   frame generation.
;
; In case of a startup crash, try:
; Preset=Vanilla
; HdtpHoloFix=Off
; RevisionSplashScreenFix=Off
; LoadFix=Off
; MonitorFix=Off
; DPIFix=Off
;
; If all else fails, try: SafeMode=On
;
; In case of an error, it would help me a LOT if you could:
; - set LogLevel=Debug,
; - reproduce the crash or the bug,
; - send the log files, the savegames and a description of how to
;   reproduce the issue to takysoft@gmail.com.
;
; PLEASE NOTE!
; The project is mainly being developed on an RX 9070 XT.
; It may not even start on an NVIDIA card
; (the last time I tested that was weeks ago).
; I do not accept NVIDIA-related issues yet!
;
; Again, please note! For certain features like FSR4, DLSS and
; ray tracing, the renderer needs to start a 64-bit companion app.
; Windows may block this companion from starting.
; As of now, the only solution I can give you is to
; disable "Smart App Control".
; Just search for "disable smart app control",
; and you'll find ways to do it.
;
; Also please note! Although this game is old, the renderer adds features
; that can be demanding for your hardware, and they are not perfectly
; optimized either. Some areas are even more demanding: the Ocean Lab,
; for example, is full of glass windows, and RT reflections on those
; decimate the FPS :D What you can try instead of turning those off:
; - select a lower Upscale setting, Performance for example,
; - try FrameGeneration together with AntiLag (Reflex on NVIDIA).
;
; - Why a separate INI file?
; - Because the game rewrites its own ini files, and loses the order of
;   the settings and every comment in them.

[SecondRevision.RevisionRenderDevice]

; DebugKey (default ScrollLock)
;   The key that opens the renderer's own menu.
;   For a keyboard without Scroll Lock, you can set this
;   to Delete or Insert, or whichever key you want.
DebugKey=ScrollLock

; Preset (default Recommended)
;   Vanilla | Recommended | INeverAskedForThis | Custom
;   Visual setting presets. Setting it to Vanilla, Recommended or
;   INeverAskedForThis makes the renderer overwrite every setting below
;   that is not marked as independent of the presets.
;
;     Vanilla             The original, stock image - or at least that is
;                         the aim. For the fans of the original look,
;                         and for slower computers.
;
;     Recommended         The game will look much nicer, but hopefully it
;                         will stay reasonably fast and well playable.
;
;     INeverAskedForThis  Pretty much the best the renderer can do:
;                         ray-traced reflections, shadows and AO,
;                         and proper 3D displacement mapping.
;                         You are going to need a strong GPU, though...
;
;     Custom              Whatever you set in the settings below.
Preset=Recommended

; GAME FIXES! (independent of the presets)
; These 5 fixes are hacky workarounds for some bugs of the original game.
; Turn them off if needed, but they should generally work okay.
;
; HdtpHoloFix (default On)   -   Off | On | Old
;   Somehow, the new HDTP models look weird when they are used as holograms.
;   This option fixes that. In case of trouble, just turn it off.
;   (The Old setting just loads the non-HDTP models for the holograms.)
HdtpHoloFix=On

; RevisionSplashScreenFix (default On)
;   The Revision mod has awesome splash screens when you load a map or a
;   savegame. They were designed for a 720p screen, though, and on higher
;   resolutions they look bad. On a 4K screen they don't even show up.
;   This fixes that, and they are shown in their awesome 720p glory :)
;   I was thinking long and hard whether to default it to On or Off, as
;   1: the Revision mod may fix this itself one day... and that may clash
;      with this fix,
;   2: it kinda involves lying to the game engine about the current
;      resolution...
;   But for now it works fantastically, so I left it On...
RevisionSplashScreenFix=On

; LoadFix (default On)
;   Sometimes the game can crash when you load a level.
;   It's rare, and it happens when you load a save after loading another
;   one, and the two saves are from two different playthroughs: some item
;   names cannot be overwritten, and the game crashes.
;   This fix forces a memory flush while a game is loading.
;   It may make loading a tiny bit slower, but also a tiny bit more stable.
;   Unless it causes issues, I recommend using it.
LoadFix=On

; DPIFix (default On)
;   In borderless windowed mode (recommended), Windows' display scaling
;   stretches the game, and it looks blurry.
;   I cannot make the game scaling-aware, so Windows' scaling needs to be
;   turned off for it. When the fix is active, it does exactly that:
;   it adds the needed Windows registry entry for the game.
;   A rather bothersome sign on the screen will then kindly ask you
;   to restart the game for it to take effect.
DPIFix=On

; MonitorFix (default On)
;   The game was not designed for multi-monitor setups.
;   With this fix, the game always opens on the main monitor.
;   (Without it... it was... crashy, and it sometimes started on
;   the wrong one, depending on the resolutions.)
;   You can switch monitors properly in the renderer's menu
;   (in a borderless window).
MonitorFix=On

;---------------------------------------------------------
; RENDERING OPTIONS
;---------------------------------------------------------
; Unless stated otherwise, these lines are only read when the preset is Custom.

; AntiAliasing (default Auto)
;   Auto | Off | FSR3 | FSR4 | DLSS | XeSS | FXAA | SMAA | SSAA4X | SSAA9X
;        | SSAA16X | MSAA2X | MSAA4X | MSAA8X | MSAA16X
;
;   You have options to experiment with, but generally you should stick to Auto.
;
;     Auto          This switches to FSR4 on compatible AMD cards, to DLSS on
;                   compatible NVIDIA cards, and to FSR3 in any other case.
;     FSR3          AMD's FSR 3.1, built into the renderer. It should work on
;                   any DirectX 12 capable card.
;     FSR4          AMD's own FSR DLLs, included. Please note: the 64-bit
;                   companion app is used to talk to these 64-bit DLLs.
;     DLSS          NVIDIA's DLSS, included. Please note: the 64-bit companion
;                   app is used to talk to these 64-bit DLLs.
;     XeSS          Intel's XeSS, on any card that runs it (most recent ones),
;                   through the 64-bit companion app as well.
;   Where FSR4, DLSS or XeSS cannot run, FSR3 is the fallback method.
;   The other options work, but they are not recommended.
AntiAliasing=Auto

; Upscale (default NativeAA)   -   NativeAA | Quality | Balanced | Performance
;   These only work with FSR3, FSR4, DLSS and XeSS.
;   NativeAA doesn't scale, it only does antialiasing (best quality).
;   Quality, Balanced and Performance draw the scene
;   1.5,     1.7 and      2 times smaller each way, for speed.
Upscale=NativeAA

; HDR (default 10bitSDR)   -   Off | 10bitSDR | On
;   Uses what the display is capable of. HDR falls back to 10bitSDR if the
;   display is not HDR capable, and 10bitSDR falls back to Off (which is
;   technically 8-bit SDR).
;   Please note: HDR is not thoroughly tested, as only my TV is HDR ready,
;   and I don't play shooters on my TV :D
;   The HDR mode is not a true re-rendering of the whole scene; it is just an
;   improvement on the darkening compensation (more on this at the shadows),
;   but it can look good, according to the tests I've run.
HDR=10bitSDR

; FsrSharpness (default 0.5)
;   Independent of the presets: they don't write this,
;   and neither should you. Don't mess with it unless you have a good reason.
FsrSharpness=0.5

; FrameGeneration (default Off)
;   Only works with FSR4 or DLSS, on compatible hardware.
;   After every rendered frame, it uses AMD's or NVIDIA's own method to create
;   another one. Pros: more FPS. Cons: input lag.
;   The input lag can be partly mitigated with AntiLag (see below).
FrameGeneration=Off

; AntiLag (default Off)
;   On | Off. The graphics driver's help against the mouse's delay: AMD Radeon
;   Anti-Lag 2 on AMD's cards (the RX 5000s and later, driver 24.7.1 or later),
;   NVIDIA Reflex on NVIDIA's. At the end of each frame the driver holds the
;   game back until the graphics card is nearly through, so the mouse is read
;   as late as it can be and the frame made from it does not wait behind
;   others. It helps where the card is the slower one; it can cost a few frames
;   a second. Where the driver has neither, it does nothing.
AntiLag=Off

; DebugReadout (default Off)   -   Off | FPS | On
;   Independent of the presets. Shows all kinds of information on the screen
;   that can be useful if you have some issues to debug, or if you are a
;   modder. The FPS mode only shows the FPS :)
DebugReadout=Off

; BorderlessWindow (default On)
;   Independent of the presets. Runs the game in a borderless window - this is
;   the modern option. It makes Alt+Tab easy, lets other apps' notifications
;   show, etc.
BorderlessWindow=On

; ExclusiveFullscreen (default Off)
;   Independent of the presets. In fullscreen, it puts the swap chain into
;   DXGI's own fullscreen state, the way the stock renderer does. Ignored
;   while BorderlessWindow is On.
ExclusiveFullscreen=Off

; GpuTextureConvert (default On)
;   Independent of the presets. Converts big palettised textures on the GPU
;   instead of the CPU. The result is identical byte for byte; this only
;   moves the work.
GpuTextureConvert=On

; GPU (default Auto)
;   Independent of the presets. Which graphics card the renderer runs on.
;   Auto selects Windows' default high-performance card (so the better one
;   on a laptop). You can give a number (1, 2, 3...) or a part of the card's
;   name too - SecondRevision.log lists the cards at its start. Auto should
;   work just fine, though.
GPU=Auto

; FPSLimit (default Auto)   -   Off | Auto | a number (60, for example)
;   Generally, you should stick with Auto. It detects your display's maximum
;   refresh rate and runs the game a bit under it, to make sure VRR
;   (FreeSync/G-Sync) works fine.
FPSLimit=Auto

; VSync (default Off)
;   Prevents screen tearing, but adds input lag. I find it obsolete with VRR,
;   but if your display lacks FreeSync/G-Sync, you may want to turn it on
;   (or get a new monitor... VRR is really a basic feature now).
VSync=Off

;---------------------------------------------------------
; VISUAL IMPROVEMENTS!
;---------------------------------------------------------
; Unless stated otherwise, these lines are only read when the preset is Custom.

; ShadowQuality (default High)
;   Off | Low | Medium | High | RTLow | RTMedium | RTHigh
;   This one adds shadows to the game. Please note that the game was not
;   designed to have shadows: adding them on top of it makes it darker.
;   We compensate for that by making the light sources stronger or, if that
;   is not enough, by reducing the shadows' strength a bit. The compensation
;   runs dynamically all the time.
;   The settings starting with 'RT' use ray tracing. These shadows look a bit
;   better, but they need RT capable hardware and the 64-bit companion app.
;   Where RT cannot be used, the fallback is the normal shadow of the same
;   level (RTLow -> Low, RTMedium -> Medium, RTHigh -> High).
;   Low, Medium and High work with the 10, 14 and 20 closest* light sources;
;   the farther half of them does not get full strength, to avoid sudden
;   flashes. Some flashing is sadly inevitable while walking; High mitigates
;   it the most. Adding even more light sources actually darkens the image
;   too much, so High really is the best option here.
;   (* see ShadowVerticalWeight below)
ShadowQuality=High

; ShadowVerticalWeight (default 2.0, clamped to 1..8)
;   Independent of the presets. The game doesn't write this, and neither
;   should you. When the shadows look for the closest light sources, a
;   light's height difference counts this many times its horizontal
;   distance, so the lights on your own floor come first. (The light sources
;   above or below you are usually upstairs or downstairs, and they should
;   not count that much.)
ShadowVerticalWeight=2.0

; AmbientOcclusion (default SSAO)   -   Off | SSAO | RT
;   Light doesn't get into corners that much - like that bouncing DVD logo on
;   your TV :D This creates those shadows, and makes the look more realistic.
;     SSAO  A good solution, it works on all computers.
;     RT    The ray-traced solution. Nicer. It needs RT capable hardware and
;           the 64-bit companion app; where it cannot run, SSAO runs instead.
AmbientOcclusion=SSAO

; WaterQuality (default High)   -   Off | Low | High
;   This one gives you much better looking 3D water, with waves.
;   It also replaces some other water sources, like fountains.
;   Raindrops interact with the waves too!
;   The High option also gives you caustics: an imitation of the light rays
;   on the bottom of the water. It looks really nice!
WaterQuality=High

; EndlessWater (default On)
;   With reflections, the water looks nice, but the end of the level is
;   painfully visible. This renders the water all the way to the horizon.
;   Originally, the level ends with an ugly black bar on the water; this
;   removes that too. Visually only: you still can't swim any farther.
EndlessWater=On

; Reflections (default High)   -   Default | High | RTHalf | RT
;   Adds some extra reflections to the game. The game has reflections on
;   mirrors and floors by default; this adds reflections to the water and to
;   glass surfaces too. Glass surfaces only get reflections in the ray tracing
;   modes. In RTHalf mode, only 1 of every 4 pixels gets its own reflection,
;   and the other 3 share it, but it looks pretty close to the RT mode, with
;   much better performance. The RT modes need RT capable hardware and the
;   64-bit companion app; without them, High runs.
Reflections=High

; Geometry (default POM)   -   Off | POM | DisplacementMapping
;   Turns flat 2D surfaces into 3D ones.
;   Please note! This only works with the "New Vision" textures, as I have
;   only included height maps for those. The reasons:
;   - only those have a resolution where this makes sense,
;   - the New Vision textures' licence allowed me to make height maps of them.
;
;   POM: Parallax Occlusion Mapping
;   This draws the flat textures with an awesome 3D depth effect, while the
;   surfaces themselves stay flat: look at them from the side, and you'll see
;   they are still 2D, with that 'sunken' 3D effect.
;
;   DisplacementMapping
;   This one actually creates new 3D geometry. I think it looks even better,
;   but one could argue, as it has to 'go down' at the edges of the textures.
;   It requires more calculations, and it lowers your FPS more.
Geometry=POM

; SoftParticles (default On)
;   Smoke, steam and fire are flat pictures (sprites) standing in the air.
;   Where one cuts into the floor or a wall, it ends in a straight line,
;   which looks really bad. This fixes that by fading the sprites out there.
SoftParticles=On

; Volumetrics (default On)
;   This gives you volumetric 3D smoke and gas.
;   They look nice, but they take a toll on your FPS.
Volumetrics=On

; DepthOfField (default On)
;   Blurs the parts of the screen that are out of focus.
;   It only takes effect in conversations! It looks nice.
DepthOfField=On

; Fire3D (default On)
;   Replaces the game's own fire and plasma effects with
;   new volumetric 3D ones. They look better.
Fire3D=On

; Grass3D (default High)   -   Off | Low | Medium | High
;   Procedurally generated grass. High has more grass :)
Grass3D=High

; Trees3D (default On)
;   Procedurally generated trees. They look nice :)
Trees3D=On

; Wind (default On)
;   A wind effect that interacts with the trees and the grass, and gives you
;   floating dust particles in the air.
Wind=On

; Fog (default Fixed)   -   Original | Fixed
;   Fog is buggy in the original game: it goes through walls, and its shading
;   is sometimes off. This fixes that.
Fog=Fixed

; ForceField (default 3D)   -   Original | 3D
;   Gives the game nice 3D force fields. They are pretty far off from the
;   original force fields, but they look nice, and I worked so-so many
;   hours on them :D
ForceField=3D

; Flashlight (default Fixed)   -   Original | Fixed | Everybody
;   Replaces the game's original, buggy flashlight with a new one that casts
;   a real beam. 'Everybody' also gives flashlights to the other soldiers
;   when they are in combat mode.
Flashlight=Fixed

;---------------------------------------------------------
; USELESS MORONIC STUFF!
;---------------------------------------------------------
; Unless stated otherwise, these lines are only read when the preset is Custom.

; GodRays (default Off)
;   Under implementation and testing, don't use!
GodRays=Off

; MotionBlur (default Off)
;   Dishonor!
MotionBlur=Off

; ChromaticAberration (default Off)
;   DISHONOR!
ChromaticAberration=Off

; Vignette (default Off)
;   Dishonor on you!
Vignette=Off

; FilmGrain (default Off)
;   Dishonor on your cow!
FilmGrain=Off

; LensFlare (default Off)   -   Off | On
;   Dishonor on your whole family!
LensFlare=Off

; Bloom (default Off)   -   Off | On
;   This one kinda looks okay...
Bloom=Off

;---------------------------------------------------------
; DEBUG STUFF
;---------------------------------------------------------

; SafeMode (default Off)
;   For a game that will not start, or falls over at once, on a machine the
;   renderer has not met: it starts with as little as can go wrong. The
;   game's fixes are off (DPIFix, RevisionSplashScreenFix, LoadFix,
;   HdtpHoloFix, MonitorFix), whatever their own keys say. The 64-bit
;   companion app is not started: DLSS, FSR 4 and XeSS run as FSR 3.1,
;   ray-traced shadows and reflections as High, ray-traced occlusion as SSAO,
;   and there is no frame generation. The display is not asked about HDR:
;   the picture is eight-bit SDR. Read at the start only; the menu shows it,
;   but cannot change it.
SafeMode=Off

; LogLevel (default Info)   -   Info | Debug
;   Debug is for finding a fault, not for playing: the game runs slower, and
;   the renderer's log takes everything it can tell - Windows, the processor
;   and the memory, the graphics cards and their drivers, the screens, every
;   program loaded into the game, the settings in effect, the Direct3D 12
;   debug layer's messages (where Windows has Graphics Tools installed), a
;   line for every frame in the first minute and after each change of level,
;   and at a crash a dump of the game's memory. To report a fault: set Debug,
;   start, make the fault happen, and send from the game's System folder
;   SecondRevision.log (SecondRevision.1.log is the run before), Revision.log,
;   SecondRevision-frames.csv and any SecondRevision-crash-*.dmp. Read at the
;   start only; the log stops itself at 1024 MB.
LogLevel=Info
